  "level": "2",
  "url": "cmse381-lec08-worksheet.html#cmse381-lec08-worksheet-the-right-way-14-2-5",
  "type": "Exercise",
  "number": "3",
  "title": "",
  "body": "   Now what does your model predict for each of the three types of cars?   # Rows represent American, European, and Japanese cars. new_origins = np.array([[0, 0], [1, 0], [0, 1]]) ...     Use (0, 0) for the omitted American reference category, (1, 0) for European, and (0, 1) for Japanese.    You can obtain all three fitted values with one call to regr.predict .    Common error: Keep the row entries aligned with the actual dummy-column order; swapping the columns swaps the European and Japanese interpretations.    The model predicts approximately 20.03 mpg for American cars, 27.60 mpg for European cars, and 30.45 mpg for Japanese cars.   new_origins = np.array([[0, 0], [1, 0], [0, 1]]) for name, prediction in zip(['American', 'European', 'Japanese'], regr.predict(new_origins)): print(f'{name}: {prediction:.2f} mpg')     "
